text:
alex: Quick question — I set .Values.secrets.name: "" in values.yaml and my Deployment's env var secretKeyRef ends up with an empty name. I expected fallback to "{{ .Release.Name }}-api-secret". Anyone know the right pattern to guard for empty string vs missing?

maya: Hmm. Can you paste the snippet you're using?

alex: Sure:

```yaml
env:
  - name: API_KEY
    valueFrom:
      secretKeyRef:
        name: {{ .Values.secrets.name | default (printf "%s-api-secret" .Release.Name) }}
        key: api_key
```

maya: If the key exists but is empty in values.yaml, default should still apply, but we had weirdness before. Two options:
- Make chart fail fast with required: `{{ required "secret name is required" .Values.secrets.name }}`
- Explicit guard: `{{- $n := .Values.secrets.name -}} {{- if or (eq $n "") (not $n) -}} ... {{- end }}`

sahil: Another simple approach: add a small helper in _helpers.tpl:

```gotemplate
{{- define "mychart.secretName" -}}
{{- if and (hasKey .Values "secrets") .Values.secrets.name -}}
{{- if ne .Values.secrets.name "" -}}
{{- .Values.secrets.name -}}
{{- else -}}
{{- printf "%s-api-secret" .Release.Name -}}
{{- end -}}
{{- else -}}
{{- printf "%s-api-secret" .Release.Name -}}
{{- end -}}
{{- end -}}
```

Then use `name: {{ include "mychart.secretName" . }}` — avoids surprises if CI injects empty strings.

deploy-bot: lint: helm lint ./charts/mychart — warnings: 1, errors: 0 :white_check_mark:

alex: Thanks! Going to add helper and also switch CI to reject explicit empty strings. :thumbsup:
